 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md)
-A shading technique that enhances depth perception by blending the sampled voxel color with a background color based on the distance from the viewer. It solves the problem of foreground/background distinction in dense volumetric data by applying a transparency falloff that dims distant objects and shifts their hue, simulating atmospheric haze. Unlike standard lighting models which compute surface reflectance, this method operates on the accumulated color along the ray, modulating it non-linearly with depth. It is distinct from [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md) (which occlude light) and [Ambient Occlusion via SDF](1631-ambient-occlusion-via-sdf.md) (which darkens crevices), as it specifically targets global depth cues rather than local geometry lighting.
+Enhances depth perception by blending sampled voxel colors with a background color based on distance from the viewer, solving the foreground/background distinction problem in dense volumetric data by applying a non-linear transparency falloff. It is distinct from [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md) (which occlude light) and [Ambient Occlusion via SDF](1631-ambient-occlusion-via-sdf.md) (which darkens crevices), as it specifically targets global depth cues rather than local geometry lighting. Choose it when simulating atmospheric haze or aerial perspective in volumetric data.
 
 ### [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md)
-Determines if a surface point is occluded from a light source by performing sphere tracing along a ray directed from the surface point toward the light. Unlike [Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md) which blends colors based on depth, this method returns a binary occlusion factor (0 for shadow, 1 for light) by checking if the SDF drops below a threshold before the ray reaches the light. It differs from [Shadow Casting Algorithm](1657-shadow-casting-algorithm.md) by leveraging the global geometric information inherent in the SDF, requiring no separate shadow map pass or rasterization overhead.
+Determines if a surface point is occluded from a light source by performing sphere tracing along a ray directed from the surface point toward the light, returning a binary occlusion factor. It differs from screen-space shadow maps by leveraging the global geometric information inherent in the SDF, requiring no separate shadow map pass or rasterization overhead. Choose it for hard, accurate shadows in SDF-based scenes.
 
 ### [Ambient Occlusion via SDF](1631-ambient-occlusion-via-sdf.md)
-Approximates the darkening of crevices and contact points by sampling the SDF along the surface normal. It detects occlusion by comparing the actual distance to nearby geometry against the expected distance if the surface were isolated. Unlike screen-space methods or shadow maps, this technique requires no separate rendering pass or auxiliary buffers, relying solely on the scene's distance function. It is computationally cheaper than full ray-marched shadows but provides a local approximation of global illumination effects.
+Approximates the darkening of crevices and contact points by sampling the SDF along the surface normal, detecting occlusion by comparing the actual distance to nearby geometry against the expected distance if the surface were isolated. Unlike screen-space methods or shadow maps, this technique requires no separate rendering pass or auxiliary buffers, relying solely on the scene's distance function. Choose it for computationally cheaper local approximation of global illumination effects.
 
 ### [Iridescent Material with Perturbation](2327-iridescent-material-with-perturbation.md)
-Renders iridescent surfaces by mapping a scalar input to a 3D color vector using a cosine palette function, simulating thin-film interference colors. It combines this base iridescent layer with specular highlights and shadowing to create a complete shading model. It differs from [Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md) by using a continuous palette function driven by perturbed normals rather than discrete distance-based color mixing, and from [Phong Shading with SDF Normals](1977-phong-shading-with-sdf-normals.md) by explicitly modeling interference-like color shifts via the palette function rather than standard diffuse/specular reflection.
+Renders iridescent surfaces by modulating a cosine-based palette function with a perturbed surface normal, simulating thin-film interference colors while combining this base color with multi-component specular highlights and directional shadowing. It differs from standard diffuse or simple specular models by generating color variation through geometric perturbation rather than wavelength-dependent physics, and from physically accurate thin-film models by using a computationally cheaper, artistic approximation driven by position and normal noise. Choose it for stylized, interference-like material effects.
 
 ### [Spherical Gaussian Shading](3169-spherical-gaussian-shading.md)
-Approximates complex lighting environments and specular reflections by decomposing incident radiance into a set of Spherical Gaussians (SGs), which are then rendered as discrete point lights or integrated into microfacet BRDF models. It solves the problem of real-time, view-dependent specular shading in volume rendering and neural radiance fields (NeRFs) by replacing expensive environment map lookups or high-order spherical harmonic integrations with efficient, analytically tractable approximations. It differs from [Phong Shading with SDF Normals](1977-phong-shading-with-sdf-normals.md) by supporting continuous, view-dependent specular lobes rather than discrete specular highlights, and from [Blinn-Phong Shading in Volume Rendering](2809-blinn-phong-shading-in-volume-rendering.md) by offering a more flexible parameterization of the specular lobe width and shape via Gaussian parameters.
+Approximates complex lighting environments and specular reflections by decomposing incident radiance into a sum of Spherical Gaussians, which are then rendered as discrete point lights or integrated into a microfacet BRDF model, solving the problem of real-time or efficient relighting in scenes with spatially variant lighting. It explicitly models specular highlights and view-dependent reflectance, differing from pure diffuse models by capturing view-dependent effects. Choose it for efficient, view-dependent specular shading in neural rendering or procedural scenes.
 
 ### [Custom Shading Models](3660-custom-shading-models.md)
-Provide a mechanism for users to define arbitrary lighting computations per-voxel or per-hit-point, bypassing standard lookup tables or fixed-function pipelines. This family solves the problem of applying non-standard, physically based, or highly stylized lighting effects to volume-rendered or ray-marched geometry. It differs from sibling methods like [Phong Shading with SDF Normals](1977-phong-shading-with-sdf-normals.md) by allowing complete algorithmic freedom rather than restricting the user to a specific analytic model, and from [Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md) by operating on lighting calculations rather than color accumulation.
+Provide a mechanism for users to define arbitrary lighting and material behaviors during volume rendering, bypassing standard lookup tables or fixed-function pipelines to allow per-voxel or per-surface-point computation of color based on user-defined logic. What sets this family apart from its siblings is that it represents the *interface* or *paradigm* for customization rather than a single mathematical lighting equation; the specific visual result depends entirely on the implementation chosen. Choose it when standard models lack the required expressiveness.
 
 ### [Neon Raymarcher Shading Model](4150-neon-raymarcher-shading-model.md)
-A real-time raymarched shading model for a procedural neon city scene, computing diffuse, specular, ambient occlusion, metallic reflection, and emissive contributions per pixel.
+A real-time shading technique designed for procedural neon city scenes, combining diffuse, specular, ambient occlusion, metallic reflection, and emissive contributions. It distinguishes itself by integrating a specific metallic reflection model weighted by a Fresnel-like term and a fixed-step shadow ray, tailored for the high-contrast, emissive aesthetic of cyberpunk environments. Choose it for stylized, high-contrast neon or cyberpunk aesthetics.
 
 ### [Diffuse Lighting Models](5343-diffuse-lighting-models.md)
-Calculate the scattering of light from a surface, providing the primary cue for shape and depth in ray-marched volume rendering and SDF rendering. Unlike specular models that highlight surface gloss or ambient models that provide uniform illumination, diffuse models compute light intensity based on the angle between the surface normal and the light direction. This family is distinct from [Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md) by integrating directional light vectors and from [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md) by assuming unobstructed light paths unless combined with shadowing techniques.
+Compute surface illumination for ray-marched geometry and SDFs by evaluating the geometric relationship between surface normals and light directions, encompassing pure diffuse models, Phong and Blinn-Phong variants, and Gouraud shading. This family is distinct from specular models that highlight surface gloss or ambient models that provide uniform illumination. Choose it for primary shape and depth cues in ray-marched volume rendering.
 
 #### Multi-Layer Shading / OmniShader
-- **Definition:** Computes all eight visualization layers simultaneously in a single forward pass, returning them as a tuple of tensors [S1, unofficial].
-- **Parameters:** `decay_factor`: float, default 0.01. Used in distance shading (though not explicitly used in the provided DistanceShader forward signature, it is registered as a buffer) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Code:**
-  ```python
-  self.cyclic_cmap = self.cyclic_cmap.roll(65, -2)
-  camera_orientation_conj = Q.conjugate(camera_orientation)[..., None, None, :]
-  lambertian_layer = self.lambertian_shader(ray_directions, surface_normals)
-  distance_layer = self.distance_shader(px_coords, surface_coords, self.decay_factor)
-  proximity_layer = self.proximity_shader(surface_distances)
-  vignette_layer = self.vignette_shader(ray_directions, pixel_frames)
-  normal_layer = self.normal_shader(surface_normals)
-  laplacian_layer = self.laplacian_layer(surface_laplacian)
-  tangent_layer = self.tangent_shader(camera_orientation_conj, ray_directions, surface_normals, self.cyclic_cmap, degree)
-  spin_layer = self.spin_shader(camera_orientation_conj, surface_normals, self.cyclic_cmap, degree)
-  return (lambertian_layer, distance_layer, proximity_layer, vignette_layer, normal_layer, laplacian_layer, tangent_layer, spin_layer)
-  ```
+Computes all eight visualization layers simultaneously in a single forward pass, returning them as a tuple of tensors: `(lambertian_layer, distance_layer, proximity_layer, vignette_layer, normal_layer, laplacian_layer, tangent_layer, spin_layer)` [S1, unofficial]. The `decay_factor` parameter (default 0.01) is registered as a buffer for distance shading [S1, unofficial]. Choose it for scientific visualization requiring simultaneous multi-layer output.
 
 #### Material Evaluation Strategy
-- **Step:** Write a material ID, the distance, and the local coordinate `p` into global variables whenever an object's distance is smaller than the stored distance. At the end of the rendering process, evaluate the material to get color, roughness, etc., and perform shading [S2, unofficial].
+Writes a material ID, the distance, and the local coordinate `p` into global variables whenever an object's distance is smaller than the stored distance. At the end of the rendering process, the material is evaluated to get color, roughness, etc., and shading is performed [S2, unofficial]. Choose it for deferred or two-pass material evaluation in ray marching.
 
 ## Background
-The integration of lighting models with Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) emerged alongside the popularization of ray marching for real-time rendering. Early implementations relied on simple Phong shading with normals estimated via finite differences [S1, S10, unofficial]. The development of efficient ambient occlusion techniques specific to SDFs, such as the linear step with exponential decay method, allowed for local illumination effects without the overhead of global illumination algorithms [S2, S5, unofficial]. More recent advancements include the use of Spherical Gaussians for efficient environment lighting approximation in neural rendering and procedural scenes [S1, S2, unofficial], and custom shading pipelines for scientific visualization [S1, unofficial].
+The integration of lighting models with Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) emerged alongside the popularization of ray marching for real-time rendering [G]. Early implementations relied on calculating surface normals using finite differences, specifically solving a linear system based on values at vertices of a tetrahedron, or via back-propagation [S1, unofficial]. The development of efficient ambient occlusion techniques specific to SDFs, such as the linear step with exponential decay method, allowed for local illumination effects without the overhead of global illumination algorithms [G]. More recent advancements include the use of Spherical Gaussians for efficient environment lighting approximation in neural rendering and procedural scenes [S1, unofficial], and custom shading pipelines for scientific visualization [S1, unofficial].
 
